1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,500
In 2025, business email fraud cost more than three billion dollars.

2
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:10,950
Now those invoices can be paid by an AI agent, without a second thought.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,050
Nobody hands a wallet to an agent that can't be stopped in time.

4
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:16,400
That is what SafePay is for.

5
00:00:19,650 --> 00:00:26,500
SafePay sits between the agent and PayPal, and checks every payment before it leaves.

6
00:00:26,550 --> 00:00:32,250
The rules are written in plain words, and signed with a key that never leaves this browser.

7
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:40,300
A real Notion invoice: paid.

8
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:46,800
The same invoice, to a look-alike domain: blocked.

9
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:51,300
A casino, inside every limit: held.

10
00:00:51,350 --> 00:00:55,400
It doesn't serve the agent's task, and a spending cap would never have seen it.

11
00:00:55,450 --> 00:00:58,050
But not everything is black or white.

12
00:00:58,650 --> 00:01:02,200
For the doubtful cases, a person decides.

13
00:01:02,250 --> 00:01:05,100
Anything over 300 dollars waits for their approval.

14
00:01:05,150 --> 00:01:09,700
One click and it's paid, signed by this browser's key:

15
00:01:09,750 --> 00:01:12,150
a stolen token approves nothing.

16
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:21,100
And if something looks wrong, one click freezes the agent,

17
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:24,400
the way a bank freezes a card.

18
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:28,500
So how do we know it holds up against a real attack?

19
00:01:29,250 --> 00:01:31,300
We put it to the test, live.

20
00:01:31,350 --> 00:01:36,950
The model writes attacks it has never seen, and SafePay judges them.

21
00:01:37,350 --> 00:01:39,450
Four out of five caught.

22
00:01:39,550 --> 00:01:44,650
One got through, and we show it as it is, with the rule that closes it.

23
00:01:45,150 --> 00:01:50,150
I sign it, apply it, and the same attacks: five out of five.

24
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:55,650
And every decision goes into a signed chain:

25
00:01:55,700 --> 00:01:59,700
if anyone alters a row, the chain breaks right there.

26
00:01:59,900 --> 00:02:03,300
And all of this, without changing tools.

27
00:02:04,950 --> 00:02:07,450
It works where the agent already is.

28
00:02:07,950 --> 00:02:11,250
It sits in front of PayPal's Agent Toolkit:

29
00:02:11,350 --> 00:02:14,750
the order exists only if SafePay allows it.

30
00:02:15,650 --> 00:02:20,150
Any agent can ask it over MCP before paying.

31
00:02:20,650 --> 00:02:25,050
And it already runs inside Impulsa, our SaaS for small businesses in Chile.

32
00:02:26,850 --> 00:02:32,700
When agents pay on their own, every PayPal payment should go through SafePay first.

33
00:02:32,750 --> 00:02:35,700
That way, saying yes to an agent stops being a leap of faith.

34
00:02:35,750 --> 00:02:38,500
We're signing up our first pilots, for free:

35
00:02:38,550 --> 00:02:41,150
scan the code and join us.

